WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Discounts and surcharges that change the answer.

2
00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:11.843
Prompt caching lets you mark a stable prefix (system prompt, tool definitions, a long document) so repeat requests read it from cache.

3
00:00:12.123 --> 00:00:15.195
On Claude at time of writing, a cache write costs 1.

4
00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:25.117
25 times the base input price for a five-minute lifetime or 2 times for one hour, and a cache read costs a small fraction of base input: 0.

5
00:00:25.397 --> 00:00:27.424
05 times on Opus 5.

6
00:00:27.704 --> 00:00:29.197
5 and Sonnet 5.

7
00:00:29.477 --> 00:00:30.587
5, 0.

8
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:32.787
1 times on most other models.

9
00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:40.427
Reads also do not count toward the input-tokens-per-minute rate limit, so caching raises throughput as well as cutting cost.

10
00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:46.189
The arithmetic says a five-minute cache pays for itself as soon as the prefix is read once.

11
00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:48.923
Batch processing trades latency for price.

12
00:00:49.203 --> 00:01:03.048
Anthropic’s Batch API processes requests asynchronously at a 50 percent discount on input and output, and the OpenAI and Gemini pricing pages also list batch rates at half their standard prices.

13
00:01:03.328 --> 00:01:09.877
If nobody is waiting on the answer (nightly classification, backfills, eval runs), batch is free money.

14
00:01:10.157 --> 00:01:12.760
On Claude, batch and caching discounts stack.

15
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.555
Surcharges exist too.

16
00:01:14.835 --> 00:01:18.269
Some prices depend on prompt length: Claude Haiku 5.

17
00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:30.645
5 charges more for prompts over 100,000 tokens, and xAI’s model table lists higher rates for prompts of 200,000 tokens or more.

18
00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:33.955
Requesting US-only inference on Claude adds a 1.

19
00:01:34.235 --> 00:01:35.685
1 times multiplier.

20
00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:40.360
Fast modes on several providers raise the price for higher output speed.

21
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:50.923
And prices change: when this lesson was reviewed, Google’s pricing page announced that Gemini 4 model prices would double on 1 December 2026.

22
00:01:51.203 --> 00:01:56.771
Keep prices in configuration with a “checked on” date, never as constants scattered through code.
